index 0000000..7b2bc14
--- /dev/null
+++ b/nemo-bridge/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+# nemo-bridge: NemoWorld residents in Veloren
+
+Three small pieces, all opt-in. With none of them configured, Veloren behaves exactly as before.
+
+| Piece | What it does |
+|---|---|
+| `server/src/sys/nemo_link.rs` | A server system. Watches **server-side** positions of a resident and other players and appends **HMAC-signed** events (`resident.met`, `landmark.together`) to an outbox file. It also resolves landmarks from world generation (real buildings of a named site) and writes `<outbox>.landmarks.json`. Off unless `NEMO_LINK_CONFIG` and `NEMO_LINK_EVENT_KEY` are set. |
+| `nemo-link-proto/` | The canonical text and HMAC. Its test uses the same vector as NemoWorld's JavaScript test. |
+| `nemo-bridge/` | A bot that plays a resident (default Lyra) as a normal character: follows the schedule from NemoWorld's roster, walks with Veloren's own pathfinder, speaks, and, with `--nemo-url`, asks NemoWorld where to go and what to answer. |
+
+## What is scripted and what is AI
+
+* The greeting, schedule lines and task announcements are **scripted**.
+* Free replies come from NemoWorld's `/veloren/talk`. If NemoWorld's local model produced it, the line starts with **`[AI]`**. If the model is off, busy, slow, over its daily allowance, or its reply is refused by NemoWorld's checks, the line is a scripted one and is **not** tagged.
+* If NemoWorld is unreachable or the credential is revoked, Lyra answers from her local script and keeps to her own schedule.
+* The bridge never decides quest progress. NemoWorld derives it only from signed server events.
+
+## Run it (all on this machine)
+
+The dev stack lives in `../dev` of the integration folder; `dev/dev.sh start [--model]` starts everything. By hand:
+
+```bash
+# 1. server: events and landmarks (copy link-config.example.json and set an absolute outbox path)
+NEMO_LINK_CONFIG=/path/link-config.json NEMO_LINK_EVENT_KEY=<shared secret, 32+ bytes> \
+  ./target/release/veloren-server-cli --non-interactive --no-auth
+
+# 2. the resident (token from NemoWorld: scopes talk,director)
+NEMO_LINK_TOKEN=<token> RUST_LOG=warn,nemo_bridge=info ./target/release/nemo-bridge \
+  --landmarks /path/outbox.jsonl.landmarks.json --nemo-url http://127.0.0.1:3100
+
+# 3. forward events (NemoWorld repo): token with scope events
+NEMO_LINK_TOKEN=<token> node scripts/veloren-relay.mjs --outbox /path/outbox.jsonl --url http://127.0.0.1:3100
+```
+
+Test players (exit 0 = pass): `nemo-bridge --probe NAME --quest` walks the whole quest; `--probe NAME` runs four
+scripted questions; `--probe NAME --say "text"` says one line and prints what it hears; `--list-sites` prints the
+named sites of the world.
+
+## Limits to know
+
+* `--no-auth` accepts any username, so **only run it on localhost**. Anyone could log in as "Lyra" when she is
+  offline and be treated as her. Run an authenticated server with a registered Lyra account for anything shared.
+* The bridge only talks to `127.0.0.1` over plain HTTP (it has no TLS). It will refuse another address.
+* Harr, the starting hamlet, has five structures. Harr has no library or pavilion, so NemoWorld's "Library" is
+  represented by Harr's house, "Peace Pavilion" by the farm field and "Meeting Hall" by the plaza.
+* Presence-based facts are what a server can prove. They show two characters stood together, not that useful work
+  was done. The first quest is a **practice** quest and awards nothing.
